---
import ToolLayout from '../../layouts/ToolLayout.astro';
import ChunkSizeCalculator from '../../components/ChunkSizeCalculator.jsx';
---
<ToolLayout title="RAG Chunk Size Calculator" description="Estimate how many chunks your document will split into for a RAG pipeline, and total tokens to embed including overlap.">
  <ChunkSizeCalculator client:load slot="tool" />

  <h2>What's the Optimal Chunk Size for RAG?</h2>
  <table>
    <thead>
      <tr><th>Content type</th><th>Suggested chunk size</th><th>Why</th></tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td>Dense technical docs / code</td><td>150–300 tokens</td><td>Smaller chunks keep precise, narrowly-scoped facts retrievable</td></tr>
      <tr><td>General prose / articles</td><td>300–500 tokens</td><td>Balances context preservation with retrieval precision</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Conversational / support transcripts</td><td>500–800 tokens</td><td>Conversational context often spans multiple exchanges</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Long-form narrative</td><td>800–1200 tokens</td><td>Meaning often depends on broader surrounding context</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <p>There's no single "correct" number — the right chunk size depends on how self-contained a unit of meaning is in your specific content, and is worth tuning against your actual retrieval quality metrics rather than a fixed default.</p>

  <h2>Why chunk size is a real design decision</h2>
  <p>Chunk size in a RAG (retrieval-augmented generation) pipeline directly affects retrieval quality — chunks too large dilute relevance and waste context window; chunks too small lose surrounding context needed to answer questions correctly. Overlap between chunks helps preserve context across boundaries, at the cost of some redundant embedding.</p>

  <h2>How to use this calculator</h2>
  <ol>
    <li>Enter your document's total size (in tokens or characters).</li>
    <li>Enter your target chunk size and overlap amount.</li>
    <li>See the estimated number of chunks and total tokens to embed.</li>
  </ol>

  <h2>Example</h2>
  <p>A 50,000-token document split into 500-token chunks with 50-token overlap produces roughly 111 chunks — more than the naive 100 you'd expect without overlap, because overlapping content is counted in multiple chunks.</p>

  <h2>Common RAG Chunking Mistakes</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Using a single fixed chunk size for all document types</strong> — dense technical docs and conversational text often benefit from different chunk sizes.</li>
    <li><strong>Ignoring overlap's embedding cost impact</strong> — higher overlap improves retrieval quality but increases the total tokens embedded and stored.</li>
    <li><strong>Chunking by pure character count</strong> without respecting sentence or paragraph boundaries, which can split meaning-bearing content awkwardly.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>When to Use This Chunk Size Calculator</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Estimating embedding costs before ingesting a large document set</li>
    <li>Planning vector database storage requirements based on expected chunk count</li>
    <li>Tuning chunk size and overlap for a specific retrieval quality target</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>RAG Chunk Size FAQ</h2>
  <p><strong>What's a good default chunk size?</strong> 200-500 tokens with 10-20% overlap is a common reasonable starting point for many text documents, though optimal values vary by content type and retrieval requirements.</p>
  <p><strong>How do I find the optimal chunk size for my RAG pipeline?</strong> Start with a reasonable default for your content type (see the table above), then evaluate retrieval quality directly — check whether retrieved chunks actually contain complete, relevant answers, and adjust size and overlap based on what you observe.</p>
  <p><strong>Does larger chunk size always mean better context?</strong> No — larger chunks dilute relevance by mixing more unrelated content together, which can actually hurt retrieval precision even though each chunk carries more surrounding context.</p>
</ToolLayout>
